      "text": "To solve this problem, we can use the relationship that the pressure (P) of a gas is inversely proportional to its volume (V) at a constant temperature. This relationship is described by the formula:\n\n\\[ P_1 V_1 = P_2 V_2 \\]\n\nwhere \\( P_1 \\) and \\( V_1 \\) are the initial pressure and volume, and \\( P_2 \\) and \\( V_2 \\) are the final pressure and volume. \n\nHere's a step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Identify the given values:**\n   - Initial pressure, \\( P_1 = 5 \\ \\text{kPa} \\)\n   - Initial volume, \\( V_1 = 2.28 \\ \\text{liters} \\)\n   - Final volume, \\( V_2 = 5.7 \\ \\text{liters} \\)\n\n2. **Substitute the given values into the equation:**\n   \\[ 5 \\ \\text{kPa} \\times 2.28 \\ \\text{liters} = P_2 \\times 5.7 \\ \\text{liters} \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\( P_2 \\):**\n   \\[ P_2 = \\frac{5 \\ \\text{kPa} \\times 2.28 \\ \\text{liters}}{5.7 \\ \\text{liters}} \\]\n   \\[ P_2 = \\frac{11.4 \\ \\text{kPa} \\cdot \\text{liters}}{5.7 \\ \\text{liters}} \\]\n\n4. **Perform the division:**\n   \\[ P_2 = 1.9837837837837836 \\ \\text{kPa} \\]\n\n5. **Round to a reasonable number of significant figures (typically two decimal places for practical purposes):**\n   \\[ P_2 \\approx 1.98 \\ \\text{kPa} \\]\n\nTherefore, the new pressure when the oxygen is moved to a 5.7 liter container at the same temperature is \\(\\boxed{1.98}\\) kPa.",
